WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
(lively music)

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.230
- These three individual
pigments can be combined

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.230
to create various shades of browns.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.390
- [Narrator] This is Kelly Dobos.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.210
- I've been formulating
products for over 20 years.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.990
I've worked for Bonne Bell,
Jergens Lotion and PURELL.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.390
Color cosmetics is one of my specialties.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.040
- [Narrator] And now she
teaches cosmetic science

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.590
at the University of Cincinnati, so...

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.282
- Today we're gonna break
down the ingredients

00:00:24.282 --> 00:00:27.123
in ILIA's True Skin Serum Foundation.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.700
Aloe leaf juice.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.980
- [Narrator] There are
over 35 ingredients listed

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.170
on this $54 foundation.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.370
- Polyricinoleate.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.020
- [Narrator] But let's
start with pigments.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.300
That's where those iron oxides come in.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.730
ILIA did not respond to The
Wall Street Journal's request

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.740
for comments on any of its ingredients

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.920
but Kelly says those
iron oxides are key here.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.860
- In foundations, we use
four primary pigments,

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.260
which are red iron
oxide, yellow iron oxide

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.960
and black iron oxide, as
well as titanium dioxide

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.550
to tint the foundation.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.310
Iron oxides are exactly like they sound.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.230
They're basically rust.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.110
Red iron oxide, of course,
is probably most familiar.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.870
Titanium dioxide is used
to tint the foundation

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.790
to different shades, but it's also added

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.350
to increased opacity.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.960
The higher coverage of a
foundation, the higher level

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.430
of titanium dioxide.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.840
See how it turns into brown

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.673
when we can mix them all together?

00:01:16.673 --> 00:01:18.720
- [Narrator] All of these
ingredients are considered safe

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.770
by the FDA.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.710
However, titanium dioxide,
which is in many foundations

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.680
in sunscreens, has recently
come under scrutiny.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.030
- Titanium dioxide can be
a carcinogen by inhalation.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.760
- [Narrator] California's
Prop 65 requires businesses

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:33.780
to warn consumers

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.150
about titanium dioxide
when it is in the form

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:39.510
of airborne particles, not
in these liquid foundations.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.520
- What we know about titanium dioxide

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.270
is that it is safe when used
in cosmetic formulations.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.510
The concern really comes
from industrial exposures

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.990
to workers who might be in
manufacturing situations.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.180
- [Narrator] Iron oxides
have a moderate safety score,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.430
according to the Environmental
Working Group's Database

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.500
on personal care product ingredients.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.500
They're found in over 7,000 foundations.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.960
- The iron oxides are
a very smooth feeling.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.790
We don't want them to
settle where you would have

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.090
to shake your foundation
every time you want to use it.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.980
- [Narrator] In order to
prevent the oxides from settling

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.200
in the foundation, chemists
will dissolve the pigments

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.760
and a liquid that has yield value.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.860
Something like this ingredient

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.190
- Yield value is the ability
to suspend a particle.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:24.180
Hand sanitizer contains a polymer
that provides yield value.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.410
You can see that it is
suspended in the solution.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.810
Another aspect of
foundations is, of course,

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:31.950
the main solvent.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.100
Water is the best known
solvent to most of us.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.380
It's a very low-cost ingredient.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.890
The ILIA foundation, however,
uses aloe leaf juice instead

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.080
of water, which has to be harvested

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.440
and processed in order to
use in cosmetic products.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.200
So that adds a significant
cost to the formulation.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.840
Aloe is about 98% water,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.921
making it a good substitute for water.

00:02:55.921 --> 00:02:57.030
(Kelly chuckling)

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.860
- [Narrator] Another
underestimated ingredient.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.630
- I think fillers often get a bad rep.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.120
They sound like they're not
doing much in a formulation

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.760
but they're really important
to balancing the feel

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.820
of a product across the
entire range of shades.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.810
- [Narrator] Fillers help the pigments

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.580
and the foundation spread evenly.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.640
Kelly says other makeup brands
have used talc as a filler.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.980
- The talc used and
cosmetics is highly refined

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.410
to remove any traces of contaminants.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.780
However, there have
been some controversies

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.410
where talc has been
contaminated with asbestos

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.650
and that has caused it to be
removed from many products.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:29.970
- [Narrator] The FDA says it continues

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.350
to test for contamination

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.390
in cosmetics containing talc.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:34.680
ILIA does not use talc.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.800
Its ingredients list rice
hull powder and boron nitride.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.440
- Boron nitride and and rice
powder are more expensive

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.630
than talc, and they have
additional properties,

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.540
like the boron nitride
provides the optical blurring.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.700
- [Narrator] This optical
blurring from the boron nitride

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.250
helps create an overall
smoothness in the face.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:54.480
- So this line represents the
particle of boron nitride.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.110
We have incoming light
hitting the particle

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.540
and it can be reflected at the same angle

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.210
or it can be reflected
at many different angles

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.330
and that's what the
optical blurring effect is.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.070
- [Narrator] There are
also skincare ingredients

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.240
on the list.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.730
- As cosmetic chemists, we
have three different strategies

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.170
for moisturizing the skin.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.480
Those are using humectants, emollients

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.580
or occlusives in a formulation.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.000
A humectant helps absorb
water like a sponge

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.660
and an emollient or occlusive
forms a protective layer

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.940
over the skin like this wax paper.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.790
If we look at the label
for the ILIA foundation,

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.740
we have hydrating ingredients.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.630
We also have emollients.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.900
Glycerin is extremely
effective at hydrating the skin

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.150
and it's also very cost effective

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.970
and it feels pretty sticky
to the touch as well.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:43.890
- [Narrator] The order
of ingredients matters.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.510
- Cosmetic ingredients are listed

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.430
in descending order of predominance.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.760
That means the greatest
concentration of ingredients

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.980
are closer to the top of the list.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.200
The ILIA Foundation contains ingredients,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.350
like niacinamide, allantoin and bisabolol.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:00.990
These are bringing
benefits to the foundation,

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:04.140
like skin tone evening,
and additional hydration.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.840
The ingredients with
skincare benefits are closer

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.690
to the middle of the list
in this formula, indicating

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.690
to me that there are
at efficacious levels.

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.040
- [Narrator] And much like lotion.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.170
- Foundations are typically
emulsion products.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.710
That means we're combining oil
and water or silicone phases.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.060
In the ILIA foundation,

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:25.080
we have polyglyceryl-2
isostearate as an emulsifier.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.380
Hydrogenated lecithin can
also be used as an emulsifier

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.810
- [Instructor] And an ordinary
ingredient also helps.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.970
- You might be wondering
why sodium chloride,

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.640
ordinary table salt is in this foundation.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.130
Well, it's really doing
a pretty important job.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.110
It helps stabilize the particles

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.210
in the emulsion so that
they don't coalesce

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.300
into larger droplets and
destabilize the formula.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.400
But it also helps lower the freezing point

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.670
so that the material's more
stable when it's transported.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.470
- [Narrator] Once the
foundation lands on your shelf,

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:53.880
preservatives are responsible

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.310
for keeping the products
safe from bacteria.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.530
- Different preservatives are efficacious

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.450
against different types of organisms.

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.700
We have yeast and molds, bacteria,

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.980
so we often have to use multiple

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.170
to achieve broad spectrum protection.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.330
Each time you dip your
finger into your product,

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:12.690
you're basically adding bacteria to it.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:17.610
And as cosmetic chemists, we
do studies to simulate that.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.670
We call that a preservative
challenge test.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:21.660
- [Narrator] There are other things

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.670
to note on the overall packaging.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:26.430
- ILIA markets itself
as a clean beauty brand.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.770
Clean beauty is not defined

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.440
in a regulatory sense in
the cosmetics industry.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.570
So many retailers and brands have come up

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.880
with their own definitions of clean beauty

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:36.990
and it tends to focus

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.450
on more naturally derived ingredients,

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.360
as well as ingredients
with known safety profiles.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.560
- [Narrator] Cosmetics are not required

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.960
to have FDA approval
before going to market,

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.180
but the FDA does provide
guidance when it comes

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.470
to product safety.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:50.793
- Safety first.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.960
I always bring up lab safety,

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.460
especially when I see a picture somewhere

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.130
of a scientist and they're
like staring at a beaker.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.740
And I'm also like, nobody stares
at a beaker up in the air.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:02.983
You put it on the table. Sorry.

00:07:02.983 --> 00:07:06.952
(interviewer chuckling)
(playful music)

